WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.868
-The closing ceremonies of the
Tokyo Olympics was Sunday night,

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:04.504
marking the end of the most
unique Olympics ever.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.973
Two of my writers,
Amber Ruffin and Jenny Hagel,

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.674
traveled to Tokyo
for the Olympics,

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:10.342
and they're here with a recap.

00:00:10.342 --> 00:00:12.679
Please welcome Jenny and Amber.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.947
-Hi! Yay! Hi, Seth!
-Hey, Seth!

00:00:14.947 --> 00:00:16.049
-Welcome back, guys.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:18.017
So, what are the takeaways
from Tokyo?

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.720
-Seth, everybody
is talking about how the U.S.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.055
won the medal count.

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:24.357
-But what no one is talking
about is the U.S.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:26.025
women's medal count.
-Yeah.

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:28.728
U.S. men won 41 medals --
not bad.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:32.064
-But the U.S. women
won 66 medals.

00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:36.402
-The U.S. women alone won more
medals than Great Britain.

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:38.538
-That's the most embarrassing
thing to happen to England

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.774
since the Oprah interview.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:44.110
-If American women broke off
and became their own country,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.312
they'd be fourth
in the medal count.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:50.483
-Plus, I bet that country would
have subsidized healthcare.

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:51.750
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.586
-This is Molly Seidel.

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:56.322
She won the bronze medal
in the women's marathon.

00:00:56.322 --> 00:01:01.928
-It was only the third marathon
she had ever run in her life.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.996
You know when the first one was?

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:06.232
At the Olympic trials.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:08.601
-This woman was a barista
at a coffee shop,

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:11.103
and she was like, "I think
I'll go to the Olympic trials,"

00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:13.239
and she did,
and then she medaled.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.942
-If I was a barista,
I wouldn't even be able

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:18.544
to medal
in getting your order right.

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.146
-This is Ashleigh Johnson.

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:22.949
She helped the U.S. women's
water polo team win gold

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:26.852
by blocking 11 shots on goal
in one match.

00:01:26.852 --> 00:01:29.855
-She successfully blocked
11 goals.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:33.126
-I haven't successfully blocked
11 spam calls.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:37.496
-And this is Allyson Felix.
Last week, she became the most

00:01:37.496 --> 00:01:41.267
decorated Olympic track athlete
in American history.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.470
-And she did it while setting up
a fund to provide childcare

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.739
for female Olympians with kids.

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:49.875
-This woman went to the
Olympics, took care of business,

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:53.880
and then also took care of like
10 other people's kids.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.849
-Also, Allyson Felix
won two Olympic medals

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:58.617
after she had a baby.

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:01.154
After I had a baby,
all I could do was this.

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:02.989
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:06.092
-The ladies
of Team USA crushed it.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:08.962
These women won medals.
-These women won medals.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:11.097
-This woman won like 300 medals.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.566
-Even the women
who didn't medal kicked ass.

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:15.968
Amber and I went to the women's
skateboarding finals,

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:20.106
where the winners were 13,
13, and 16 years old.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.941
-But you know
who came in fourth?

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:25.044
A 34-year-old American woman!

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.413
That woman was only
eight years younger

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:30.216
than the other three girls'
ages combined.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:34.520
-A 34-year-old woman went to
the Olympics and did this.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:44.530 line:0%
♪♪

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.733
-I can't do that, and I'm 34.

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.069
-You're 34?
-Yes.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.238
And I have been
for several years.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:56.542
-Look, the point we're trying to
make is that Team USA did great!

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:59.378
But they did so in large part
because of the hard work

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:01.680
and talent of American women.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.783
-And also probably
because we were there.

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:04.917
-Yeah.

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.018
-That's fantastic.

00:03:06.018 --> 00:03:07.420
Can you give us
some more highlights?

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.488
-Sorry, we can't.
We have bobsled practice.

00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:12.225
-What?
-Beijing is in like five months.

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:14.260
And the way things
are going for American women,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.895
we figure
we can at least get bronze.

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.397
-Amber and Jenny, everyone.

